# Build context, shared by BOTH Dockerfiles in this repo.
#
# ONE FILE, BECAUSE THERE CAN ONLY BE ONE. Per-Dockerfile ignore files
# (Dockerfile.blackwell.dockerignore) are a BuildKit feature, and this machine's
# docker has BuildKit enabled but no buildx component, so builds run on the
# classic builder -- which reads `.dockerignore` and nothing else. Two ignore
# files that only one builder reads is exactly the kind of silently-inert
# configuration this project keeps paying for.
#
# `Dockerfile` (CUDA 11.8) is toolchain-only and COPYs nothing, so the context
# is irrelevant to it beyond a few seconds of tar. `Dockerfile.blackwell`
# (CUDA 12.8) COPIES THE WHOLE REPO IN -- that is the point of it: the tag pins
# a commit and a rented machine runs `docker run` with no clone and no build.
#
# So what is excluded here is exactly the artifact trees -- ~120 GB of past runs
# and archives, none of which is an INPUT to anything the image does. A seed is
# a flat uniform spin-up: empty topoFile, empty inFile, no sounding, no terrain,
# no land cover. data/grid* is carried anyway (4 MB) because the same image also
# runs corpus cases, which do read it.
.git
.gitignore
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# --- artifact trees ---------------------------------------------------------
# runs/ is 96 GB of output/ and window/ dumps -- and it also holds the .in TEMPLATES every
# case is built from, which are SOURCE living in an artifact tree. Excluding the tree ate
# them, and stage 2 then died with FileNotFoundError on runs/g30_base/base.in on EVERY case
# of all eight machines: 81 cases, 0 produced, ~11 min of 64 GPUs. The dry run could not
# see it, because the stub replaces the whole case path.
#
# MEASURED, and it REFINES the note recorded on 2026-08-31: a `!` re-include DOES work
# under the classic builder -- but only if EVERY parent directory is itself re-included by
# name. `runs` + `!runs/g30_base` + `!runs/g30_base/base.in` restores the file (verified in
# a built image); `runs/**` + `!runs/*/base.in` does NOT, because the glob never restores
# the intermediate directory. The earlier seeds case was the second shape.
runs
!runs/g30_base
!runs/g30_base/base.in
!runs/g24_base
!runs/g24_base/base.in
figures
pairs
pairs_npz
pairs_npz_retired
corpus-out

# --- THE SEED LIBRARY IS BAKED IN, AND ONLY THE THREE FILES A CASE NEEDS ------------------
# A corpus case restarts from a seed chosen PER CASE by bin/pick_seed.py, which reads every
# seed's manifest.json (for `achieved`) and stationarity.json (for the gate state) before
# deciding -- so the whole library has to be present, not a subset, and which seed a case
# wants is not knowable in advance.
#
# BAKED IN RATHER THAN MOUNTED, deliberately. The library is 2.1 GB of restarts and it is
# IMMUTABLE: it is the output of a finished run, versioned by the same commit as the code
# that reads it. Mounting it would mean copying 2.1 GB onto each of eight rented boxes by
# hand -- eight chances to copy the wrong library, or a partial one, onto a machine that
# would then generate plausible cases off it. In the image the tag pins both the code and
# the seeds together, `docker pull` is the only transfer, and a machine cannot start with
# the wrong library because there is nowhere else for it to come from.
#
# The restarts are not in git (they live on Hugging Face, see docs/getting-started/data.md);
# bin/fetch_assets.sh puts them at seeds/*/return/seed_restart.nc before the image is built.
#
# The rest of return/ -- acceptance.txt, budget.json, rotation_check, turb_alive,
# direction_drift, seed_report -- is evidence about how the seed was made and no case reads
# any of it. Those stay out. AND THE EXCLUSION IS AT FILE LEVEL, NOT DIRECTORY LEVEL:
# `seeds/*/return` followed by `!seeds/*/return/seed_restart.nc` DOES NOT WORK -- MEASURED
# on this docker (classic builder, DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0), a `!` cannot re-include a file whose
# parent directory is excluded, because the walker never descends. The build failed on its
# own "30 seed restarts" assertion, which is the assertion earning its place.
#
# Patterns are ONE LEVEL: `*` does not cross `/`. An earlier library kept smoke dumps one
# level deeper (`<seed>/smoke_aside/FE_SMOKE.*`) and 560 MB rode into an image that way; the
# Dockerfile's `find` over the whole tree is what catches a pattern being wrong.
seeds/*/output
seeds/*/window
seeds/*/FE_*
seeds/*/*.log
seeds/*/return/*.log
seeds/*/return/*.txt
seeds/*/return/budget.json
seeds/*/return/rotation_check.json
seeds/*/return/seed_report.json
seeds/*/return/turb_alive.json

# --- data: the small model grids stay, the archives go ----------------------
data/raw
data/hrrr
data/smokelib
data/case_grids

# --- results: the JSON/txt record stays, the arrays go ----------------------
results/**/*.npz
results/**/*.nc

# --- FastEddy: NOT taken from the host. The image runs fasteddy/fetch.sh itself, which
# fetches NCAR v5.0.1, applies fasteddy/patches/ and verifies fasteddy/MANIFEST.sha256, so
# the tree inside the image is pinned by files that ARE in git rather than by whatever
# checkout happens to be on the build host.
FastEddy-model-5.0.1

# --- prebuilt binaries: the image compiles its own --------------------------
lib/liblpdm.so
